# Discoolver CMS - Coding Rules

## Project Overview
This is a Next.js 14+ CMS application for managing Discoolver content, using TypeScript, Server Actions, and the App Router.

## Tech Stack
- **Framework**: Next.js 14+ (App Router)
- **Language**: TypeScript (strict mode)
- **UI Library**: React 18+, shadcn/ui
- **Styling**: Tailwind CSS
- **State Management**: React Query (TanStack Query)
- **API**: RESTful API with centralized client

## Project Structure

### Directory Organization
```
src/
├── app/                    # Next.js App Router
│   ├── (auth)/            # Auth routes (login, signup, etc.)
│   ├── (protected)/       # Protected routes (dashboard, etc.)
│   ├── actions/           # Server Actions (API layer)
│   └── api/               # API routes (minimal use)
├── components/
│   ├── auth/              # Authentication components
│   ├── common/            # Shared components
│   ├── layout/            # Layout components (sidebar, nav, etc.)
│   ├── providers/         # Context providers
│   └── ui/                # shadcn/ui components
├── config/                # Configuration files
├── hooks/                 # Custom React hooks
├── lib/                   # Utility libraries
└── types/                 # TypeScript type definitions
```

## Coding Conventions

### TypeScript
- **Always use TypeScript**: No JavaScript files except configs
- **Type-first approach**: Define types in separate files under `src/types/`
- **Export types through index**: Use barrel exports in `types/index.ts`
- **Explicit return types**: Always specify return types for functions
- **Interface over type**: Prefer `interface` for object shapes
- **Avoid `any`**: Use `unknown` if type is truly unknown

### Server Actions

#### Location and Structure
- Place all server actions in `src/app/actions/`
- Group by domain (e.g., `auth.ts`, `business.ts`, `category.ts`)
- Always start with `"use server";` directive
- Export through `actions/index.ts` for cleaner imports

#### Pattern
```typescript
"use server";

import { apiClient, apiClientV2 } from "@/lib/api-client";
import { getAuthToken } from "@/lib/auth";
import { ApiError } from "@/types/api";
import type { ReturnType } from "@/types";

/**
 * Description of what this action does
 * @param param1 - Description
 * @param param2 - Description
 * @returns Description
 */
export async function actionName(
  param1: Type1,
  param2: Type2
): Promise<ReturnType> {
  try {
    const token = await getAuthToken();
    
    if (!token) {
      throw new Error("Authentication required");
    }

    const endpoint = `/path/${param1}/${param2}`;
    const response = await apiClient.get<ReturnType>(endpoint, { token });

    return response;
  } catch (error) {
    if (error instanceof ApiError) {
      throw error;
    }
    if (error instanceof Error) {
      throw error;
    }
    throw new Error("An unexpected error occurred");
  }
}
```

### API Client Usage

#### Use Centralized API Clients
- **v1 API**: Use `apiClient` from `@/lib/api-client`
- **v2 API**: Use `apiClientV2` from `@/lib/api-client`
- **Never use raw `fetch`** in actions unless absolutely necessary
- API clients handle: authentication headers, error handling, auto-logout

#### Available Methods
```typescript
apiClient.get<T>(endpoint, { token })
apiClient.post<T>(endpoint, body, { token })
apiClient.put<T>(endpoint, body, { token })
apiClient.patch<T>(endpoint, body, { token })
apiClient.delete<T>(endpoint, { token })
```

### Error Handling

#### API Errors
- Use `ApiError` class for structured errors
- Always catch and re-throw `ApiError` instances
- Provide meaningful error messages
- Never swallow errors silently

#### Pattern
```typescript
try {
  // API call
} catch (error) {
  if (error instanceof ApiError) {
    throw error; // Re-throw API errors
  }
  if (error instanceof Error) {
    throw error;
  }
  throw new Error("Descriptive error message");
}
```

### Types and Interfaces

#### File Organization
- One domain per file (e.g., `business.ts`, `category.ts`, `user.ts`)
- Export all types through `types/index.ts`
- Co-locate related types (request, response, params)

#### Naming Conventions
```typescript
// Entity types
export interface Business { }
export interface Category { }

// Response types
export interface BusinessListResponse { }
export type CategoryListResponse = Category[];

// Request/Query params
export interface BusinessQueryParams { }

// Domain-specific subtypes
export interface BusinessLocation { }
export interface BusinessSEO { }
```

### Components

#### File Naming
- Use kebab-case for files: `login-form.tsx`, `app-sidebar.tsx`
- Component name should match file name (PascalCase)

#### Component Structure
```typescript
import { /* imports */ } from "library";
import type { /* types */ } from "@/types";

interface ComponentProps {
  prop1: Type1;
  prop2?: Type2; // Optional props with ?
}

export function ComponentName({ prop1, prop2 }: ComponentProps) {
  // Component logic
  return (
    // JSX
  );
}
```

#### UI Components
- Use shadcn/ui components from `@/components/ui`
- Don't modify shadcn components directly
- Create wrapper components if customization needed

### Imports

#### Order
1. External libraries (React, Next.js, etc.)
2. Internal components
3. Internal utilities/libs
4. Types (use `import type` for type-only imports)
5. Styles/assets

#### Aliases
- Use `@/` for all absolute imports
- Never use relative imports across domains
```typescript
// ✅ Good
import { getAuthToken } from "@/lib/auth";
import type { Business } from "@/types/business";

// ❌ Bad
import { getAuthToken } from "../../lib/auth";
```

### Naming Conventions

#### Variables and Functions
- `camelCase` for variables and functions
- Descriptive names, avoid abbreviations
- Boolean variables: prefix with `is`, `has`, `should`

#### Constants
- `UPPER_SNAKE_CASE` for true constants
```typescript
const API_BASE_URL = "https://api.discoolver.com";
const MAX_RETRY_ATTEMPTS = 3;
```

#### Server Actions
- Verb-first naming: `getBusinesses`, `createCategory`, `updateUser`
- Use common CRUD verbs: `get`, `create`, `update`, `delete`, `list`

### Documentation

#### JSDoc Comments
- Required for all exported functions
- Include `@param` for parameters
- Include `@returns` for return values
- One-line description of what the function does

```typescript
/**
 * Fetches SEO information for a specific business
 * @param id - Business ID
 * @param language - Language code (e.g., 'es', 'en')
 * @returns Business SEO data including title, description, and tags
 */
export async function getBusinessSEO(
  id: number,
  language: string
): Promise<BusinessSEO> {
  // Implementation
}
```

## API Integration

### Authentication
- Token stored in cookies via `getAuthToken()`
- Use `CMSAuthorization: Bearer ${token}` header
- Auto-logout on authorization exceptions (handled by api-client)

### API Versions
- Base URL: `NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL` env var (e.g. `https://api.discoolver.com`)
- v1: `${NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL}/cms/v1` (use `apiClient`)
- v2: `${NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL}/cms/v2` (use `apiClientV2`)

### Response Handling
- Always type API responses
- Use interfaces for response shapes
- Handle loading and error states in components

## Code Style

### Formatting
- 2 spaces indentation
- Single quotes for strings (unless JSX)
- Semicolons required
- Trailing commas in multi-line objects/arrays
- Max line length: 80-100 characters (flexible)

### React Patterns
- Prefer function components over class components
- Use hooks for state and side effects
- Keep components small and focused
- Extract logic to custom hooks when reusable

### Async/Await
- Use async/await over promises
- Always handle errors with try/catch
- Never use `.then()` chains in new code

## Best Practices

### Performance
- Use React Query for data fetching and caching
- Implement proper loading states
- Lazy load heavy components when possible

### Security
- Never expose sensitive data in client components
- Use server actions for all API calls
- Validate all user inputs
- Sanitize data before rendering

### Accessibility
- Use semantic HTML elements
- Include proper ARIA labels
- Ensure keyboard navigation works
- Maintain proper color contrast

### Testing
- Write tests for critical business logic
- Test error scenarios
- Mock API calls in tests

## Git Workflow

### Commits
- Use conventional commits format
- Keep commits atomic and focused
- Write descriptive commit messages

### Branches
- Create feature branches from `main`
- Use descriptive branch names: `feature/seo-integration`, `fix/auth-redirect`

## Environment Variables

### Required
- `NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL`: API base URL (defaults to https://api.discoolver.com)

### Naming
- Public (client-side): `NEXT_PUBLIC_*`
- Server-only: No prefix

## Additional Notes

- Always run linter before committing
- Keep dependencies up to date
- Document breaking changes
- Follow Next.js best practices and conventions
- Prioritize code readability over cleverness

